1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:03,547
Two plus three is the
same as three plus two.

2
00:00:03,547 --> 00:00:06,384
But five minus two is not two minus five.

3
00:00:06,784 --> 00:00:08,139
So what about sets?

4
00:00:08,139 --> 00:00:10,558
Does the order ever change the answer?

5
00:00:11,008 --> 00:00:14,211
Aarav asked this while
revising sets after class.

6
00:00:14,561 --> 00:00:17,019
Is A union B the same as B union A?

7
00:00:17,019 --> 00:00:19,837
And what if he mixes
union with intersection?

8
00:00:20,337 --> 00:00:22,468
Sets follow a short list of laws.

9
00:00:22,468 --> 00:00:25,572
Learn them once, and long
expressions become short.

10
00:00:26,222 --> 00:00:27,420
First, order.

11
00:00:27,420 --> 00:00:30,627
A union B collects
everything in A or in B.

12
00:00:30,627 --> 00:00:33,814
B union A collects
exactly the same elements.

13
00:00:34,214 --> 00:00:36,010
So union is commutative.

14
00:00:36,010 --> 00:00:37,976
The order does not matter.

15
00:00:37,976 --> 00:00:40,197
Intersection is commutative too.

16
00:00:40,597 --> 00:00:41,653
Now grouping.

17
00:00:41,653 --> 00:00:44,118
Join A and B first, then add C.

18
00:00:44,118 --> 00:00:46,800
Or join B and C first, then add A.

19
00:00:47,200 --> 00:00:50,692
Either way, you collect
every element of all three.

20
00:00:50,692 --> 00:00:52,407
So union is associative.

21
00:00:52,407 --> 00:00:54,021
The brackets can move.

22
00:00:54,021 --> 00:00:56,142
Intersection is associative too.

23
00:00:56,792 --> 00:00:58,602
But recall episode eight.

24
00:00:58,602 --> 00:01:01,721
A minus B and B minus
A are different sets.

25
00:01:02,371 --> 00:01:04,064
Next, the special sets.

26
00:01:04,064 --> 00:01:07,984
The empty set adds nothing,
so A union the empty set is A.

27
00:01:08,384 --> 00:01:12,035
And A union U is U, because
U already holds everything.

28
00:01:12,435 --> 00:01:14,315
For intersection, it flips.

29
00:01:14,315 --> 00:01:17,310
A intersection the empty
set is the empty set.

30
00:01:17,310 --> 00:01:18,909
A intersection U is A.

31
00:01:19,309 --> 00:01:20,848
And a set with itself.

32
00:01:20,848 --> 00:01:23,749
A union A is A, and A intersection A is A.

33
00:01:23,749 --> 00:01:25,708
Doing it twice changes nothing.

34
00:01:25,708 --> 00:01:28,003
These are called the idempotent laws.

35
00:01:28,653 --> 00:01:31,898
Now the big one, where
union and intersection mix.

36
00:01:31,898 --> 00:01:33,315
The distributive law.

37
00:01:33,715 --> 00:01:39,317
It says: A intersection, B union C, equals
A intersection B, union A intersection

38
00:01:39,317 --> 00:01:40,217
C.

39
00:01:40,147 --> 00:01:42,134
Let's test it with shading.

40
00:01:42,134 --> 00:01:43,454
Left side first.

41
00:01:43,454 --> 00:01:44,839
Shade B union C.

42
00:01:44,839 --> 00:01:47,330
Then keep only the part inside A.

43
00:01:48,080 --> 00:01:49,554
Now the right side.

44
00:01:49,554 --> 00:01:51,233
Shade A intersection B.

45
00:01:51,233 --> 00:01:53,127
Then add A intersection C.

46
00:01:53,877 --> 00:01:56,294
Both sides shade the same three pieces.

47
00:01:56,294 --> 00:01:59,809
The picture works for any
sets, so the law always holds.

48
00:02:00,459 --> 00:02:02,826
It works the other way round too.

49
00:02:02,826 --> 00:02:07,652
A union, B intersection C, equals
A union B, intersection A union C.

50
00:02:08,252 --> 00:02:09,326
Side by side.

51
00:02:09,326 --> 00:02:12,469
In numbers, multiplication
spreads over addition.

52
00:02:12,469 --> 00:02:17,174
Two times, three plus four, is two
times three, plus two times four.

53
00:02:17,574 --> 00:02:20,515
But addition does not
spread over multiplication.

54
00:02:20,515 --> 00:02:23,221
Two plus, three times four, is fourteen.

55
00:02:23,221 --> 00:02:26,370
Two plus three, times
two plus four, is thirty.

56
00:02:26,970 --> 00:02:28,039
Now you try.

57
00:02:28,039 --> 00:02:32,723
For sets, does union spread over
intersection, the same way intersection

58
00:02:32,723 --> 00:02:34,156
spreads over union?

59
00:02:34,156 --> 00:02:35,486
Pause and decide.

60
00:02:38,686 --> 00:02:39,586
Yes!

61
00:02:39,209 --> 00:02:42,203
With sets, both
distributive laws are true.

62
00:02:42,203 --> 00:02:45,525
That is one way sets are
friendlier than numbers.

63
00:02:46,175 --> 00:02:47,179
Remember this.

64
00:02:47,179 --> 00:02:51,698
Order and brackets never matter, and
each operation spreads over the other.

65
00:02:52,398 --> 00:02:56,936
Last, the laws for complements, named
after the mathematician Augustus

66
00:02:56,936 --> 00:02:57,843
De Morgan.

67
00:02:58,243 --> 00:02:59,158
Step one.

68
00:02:59,158 --> 00:03:03,686
The complement of A union B is
everything outside both circles.

69
00:03:04,436 --> 00:03:05,336
Step two.

70
00:03:05,315 --> 00:03:07,300
Outside A is A complement.

71
00:03:07,300 --> 00:03:09,284
Outside B is B complement.

72
00:03:09,284 --> 00:03:12,148
Outside both is where these two overlap.

73
00:03:12,898 --> 00:03:13,863
Step three.

74
00:03:13,863 --> 00:03:18,979
So the complement of A union B equals
A complement intersection B complement.

75
00:03:19,729 --> 00:03:24,109
In the same way, the complement of
A intersection B equals A complement

76
00:03:24,109 --> 00:03:25,458
union B complement.

77
00:03:25,458 --> 00:03:28,253
The complement flips
union into intersection.

78
00:03:28,903 --> 00:03:29,811
Quick one.

79
00:03:29,811 --> 00:03:34,925
Is the complement of A union B the same
as A complement union B complement?

80
00:03:34,925 --> 00:03:36,208
Pause and think.

81
00:03:39,408 --> 00:03:40,308
No!

82
00:03:39,909 --> 00:03:44,774
A complement union B complement is
the complement of A intersection B.

83
00:03:44,774 --> 00:03:48,245
It also covers the parts
inside only one circle.

84
00:03:48,895 --> 00:03:50,631
Now, one worked example.

85
00:03:50,981 --> 00:03:52,343
U is one to eight.

86
00:03:52,343 --> 00:03:54,568
A is one, two, three and four.

87
00:03:54,568 --> 00:03:56,841
B is three, four, five and six.

88
00:03:56,841 --> 00:03:58,625
Check De Morgan's first law.

89
00:03:59,125 --> 00:04:00,025
Step one.

90
00:04:00,012 --> 00:04:03,039
A union B is the numbers one up to six.

91
00:04:03,789 --> 00:04:04,689
Step two.

92
00:04:04,636 --> 00:04:07,405
Its complement in U is seven and eight.

93
00:04:08,155 --> 00:04:09,055
Step three.

94
00:04:09,029 --> 00:04:11,958
A complement is five,
six, seven and eight.

95
00:04:11,958 --> 00:04:14,840
B complement is one, two, seven and eight.

96
00:04:15,590 --> 00:04:16,490
Step four.

97
00:04:16,417 --> 00:04:19,508
The numbers in both of
them are seven and eight.

98
00:04:20,258 --> 00:04:21,158
Step five.

99
00:04:21,128 --> 00:04:23,339
Both sides give seven and eight.

100
00:04:23,339 --> 00:04:25,190
De Morgan's law checks out.

101
00:04:25,840 --> 00:04:27,306
Now, the exam trap.

102
00:04:27,656 --> 00:04:31,936
Many students move brackets freely,
even when union and intersection are

103
00:04:31,936 --> 00:04:32,836
mixed.

104
00:04:32,928 --> 00:04:33,828
Test it.

105
00:04:33,681 --> 00:04:36,167
A is one, B is two, C is three.

106
00:04:36,167 --> 00:04:37,733
The left side is one.

107
00:04:37,733 --> 00:04:39,446
The right side is empty.

108
00:04:39,446 --> 00:04:43,066
Brackets move freely only when
the operation is the same.

109
00:04:43,716 --> 00:04:45,334
Where is this asked?

110
00:04:45,334 --> 00:04:50,980
In PUC board exams, as: verify a law,
or prove an identity like De Morgan's.

111
00:04:50,980 --> 00:04:55,062
And in JEE Main, as: which
set statement is not true.

112
00:04:55,662 --> 00:04:56,967
Exam level now.

113
00:04:56,967 --> 00:04:58,438
Simplify this set.

114
00:04:58,438 --> 00:04:59,799
Pause and solve.

115
00:05:05,199 --> 00:05:06,718
The answer is C.

116
00:05:06,718 --> 00:05:07,618
Just B.

117
00:05:08,066 --> 00:05:10,328
Both brackets contain union B.

118
00:05:10,328 --> 00:05:14,520
So take B outside, using the
distributive law in reverse.

119
00:05:15,020 --> 00:05:17,918
That leaves A intersection
A complement, union B.

120
00:05:18,418 --> 00:05:21,398
A intersection A complement
is the empty set.

121
00:05:21,398 --> 00:05:23,989
And the empty set union B is just B.

122
00:05:24,589 --> 00:05:26,050
Here is your recap.

123
00:05:26,050 --> 00:05:27,551
Screenshot this card.

124
00:05:30,751 --> 00:05:33,588
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

125
00:05:33,988 --> 00:05:34,888
One.

126
00:05:34,570 --> 00:05:37,940
Which law says A union B equals B union A?

127
00:05:38,840 --> 00:05:39,740
Two.

128
00:05:39,334 --> 00:05:41,772
What is A intersection the empty set?

129
00:05:42,672 --> 00:05:43,572
Three.

130
00:05:43,293 --> 00:05:47,522
Write the complement of A
intersection B, using De Morgan's law.

131
00:05:48,622 --> 00:05:53,220
Next time: twenty five like tea,
eighteen like coffee, and ten like both.

132
00:05:53,220 --> 00:05:55,214
So how many students are there?

133
00:05:55,214 --> 00:05:56,737
We count it with sets.
